# Requests that show OpenIDProviderApp working: the discovery document, the published keys, an access token
# from the client credentials grant and introspection of that token, then a sign-in on the login page with
# the authorization code flow and the exchange of its code for tokens.
#
# Start the provider first (dotnet run), then open this file in Visual Studio, JetBrains Rider or VS Code
# (REST Client extension) and send each request in turn. Trust the development HTTPS certificate once so
# the client accepts it:
#     dotnet dev-certs https --trust

# The provider's address: its launch profile's URL, which is also the issuer it writes into every token.
@host = https://localhost:5001

# A confidential client registered for the client credentials grant only; "secret" is the demo
# secret whose SHA-512 hash appsettings.Development.json declares, read at startup in Development (the
# environment of the launch profile `dotnet run` uses).
@clientId = console_client
@clientSecret = secret

# After running the token request below, paste its access_token here to run the introspection request.
@accessToken = paste-the-access_token-here

# For the code exchange at the end of this file: paste the code of a sign-in here. code_challenge in the
# authorization address there is the S256 hash of @codeVerifier (the example pair of RFC 7636, appendix B).
# It proves to the token endpoint that the client exchanging the code is the one that asked for it, so a
# real client generates a new verifier for every sign-in.
@code = paste-the-code-here
@codeVerifier = dBjftJeZ4CVP-mB92K27uhbUJU1p1r_wW1gFWFOEjXk

### Discovery document
# Answers 200 with the issuer https://localhost:5001 and the endpoint addresses used below: token_endpoint,
# jwks_uri and introspection_endpoint.
GET {{host}}/.well-known/openid-configuration

### Published keys
# Answers 200 with the public half of each signing key (kty, n, e), never any private material.
GET {{host}}/.well-known/jwks

### Access token from the client credentials grant
# Answers 200 with access_token, token_type Bearer and expires_in. The token is addressed to this provider
# (its aud is the issuer), so no API registered with the provider accepts it; the request names no scope,
# so the token carries none.
POST {{host}}/connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=client_credentials&client_id={{clientId}}&client_secret={{clientSecret}}

### Introspection of that token
# The endpoint answers only an authenticated client, so the same client sends its own credentials; the
# token was issued to it, so it needs no further permission. Answers 200 with "active": true and the
# token's client_id and exp; an expired or unknown token answers 200 with "active": false.
POST {{host}}/connect/introspect
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

token={{accessToken}}&client_id={{clientId}}&client_secret={{clientSecret}}

### Tokens for a code from signing in as test_client
# Signing in with the authorization code flow, as test_client, the interactive client that
# appsettings.Development.json declares with the same demo secret "secret". The sign-in happens in a
# browser, so open this address there:
#
#     https://localhost:5001/connect/authorize?client_id=test_client&response_type=code&scope=openid%20profile%20email&redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2F127.0.0.1%2Fcallback&code_challenge=E9Melhoa2OwvFrEMTJguCHaoeK1t8URWbuGJSstw-cM&code_challenge_method=S256&state=demo
#
# The provider shows its login page; sign in there with the login and password given to dotnet new as
# --user and --password (Passw0rd! when --password was left out). The browser is then sent to
# http://127.0.0.1/callback?state=demo&code=...&iss=..., an address nothing in this project serves, so it
# shows an error page: copy the code parameter from the address bar into @code at the top of this file and
# send this request within a minute, the lifetime of a code.
#
# Answers 200 with id_token, access_token, token_type Bearer and expires_in. The id_token names the user in
# its sub claim. redirect_uri repeats the one of the authorization request, as the token endpoint requires.
POST {{host}}/connect/token
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

grant_type=authorization_code&code={{code}}&redirect_uri=http%3A%2F%2F127.0.0.1%2Fcallback&code_verifier={{codeVerifier}}&client_id=test_client&client_secret=secret
